+---
+id: task-005-fix-single-use-coupon-not-returned-on-cancel-confirm-free-shipping-at-50-already-fixed
+type: task
+title: "fix single-use coupon not returned on cancel; confirm free-shipping-at-50 already fixed"
+status: pending
+---
+
+Two reports came in about the order service. Handled each against the business rules in
+`README.md`.
+
+## 1. Customer (Paolo): one-time welcome code refused as already used, after cancelling and
+   reordering
+
+Paolo placed an order with his single-use welcome coupon, cancelled it the same day (wrong size),
+then placed a corrected order with the same code — refused as already used.
+
+Cause: `OrderService.cancel` in `src/orders.ts` called `this.#coupons.giveBack(order.coupon,
+order.customer)`, passing the *customer's name*. But `Coupons.use` (called from `OrderService.place`)
+records usage keyed by the *order id* (`this.#usedBy.set(coupon.code, order)` where `order` is the
+order id passed in as `id`). `Coupons.giveBack` only releases the coupon when its stored value matches
+the identifier passed in (`this.#usedBy.get(canonical) === order`), so passing the customer name
+instead of the order id never matched, and a cancelled order's single-use coupon was never freed —
+contradicting the README ("Cancelling it returns its units and gives back its coupon, if the coupon
+was single-use.").
+
+Fix: `src/orders.ts` — `cancel()` now calls `this.#coupons.giveBack(order.coupon, order.id)`, matching
+the identifier `use()` stores.
+
+Test added: `test/coupons.test.ts` — "gives a single-use coupon back when the order that used it is
+cancelled, so it can be used again": places an order with a single-use coupon, cancels it, asserts
+`coupons.usedBy(...)` is `undefined`, then places a second order with the same coupon and asserts the
+discount applies. Red before the fix (coupon stayed marked as used), green after.
+
+## 2. Support: customer charged €4.90 delivery on an order whose goods came to exactly €50.00
+
+Investigated against `src/shipping.ts` (`shippingCost`) and `src/pricing.ts` (`goodsCents`), which
+together implement the README's free-shipping rule ("goods come to €50.00 or more, after every
+discount and before VAT").
+
+Finding: this exact boundary bug already exists as task-002
+(`docs/memory/task/task-002-fix-free-shipping-boundary-at-exactly-50-euro.md`) and was already fixed
+in an earlier change to `src/shipping.ts` (`goods >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM_CENTS`, not `>`), with a
+passing regression test in `test/orders.test.ts` ("ships free when the goods come to exactly the
+free-shipping amount"). `npm test` confirms this test currently passes.
+
+I also checked the case this new report doesn't rule out — a coupon bringing the goods down to
+exactly €50.00, rather than no discount at all — since coupon proration (`allocate` in `src/money.ts`)
+could in principle introduce a rounding mismatch at the boundary. It doesn't: `allocate` guarantees its
+shares sum to the coupon total exactly, so `goodsCents` (gross − volume discount − coupon) lands on
+exactly the same value whether or not a coupon is involved. Confirmed with a probe and added as a
+regression test: `test/coupons.test.ts` — "ships free when a coupon brings the goods down to exactly
+the free-shipping amount" (6 × €10.00 mug with a €10.00 fixed coupon = exactly €50.00 of goods).
+Characterization test: it already passed before this change, confirming the behaviour the customer is
+missing is not currently reproducible.
+
+No change made to `src/shipping.ts` or `src/pricing.ts` for this report: the code already matches the
+README. The customer-facing answer is that this should already be fixed; if she was charged on an
+order placed before the earlier fix shipped, that explains the one-off charge, and placing the same
+order today would ship free. If support can get the order date or ID, that would confirm whether it
+predates the fix or whether something else is going on that this investigation didn't reproduce.
+
+## Verification
+
+`npm test` — 21/21 passing (1 new test was red before the `src/orders.ts` fix, green after; 1 new
+test for report 2 was green before and after, as expected for already-fixed behaviour).
